{"name":"In Good Taste","hex":"#b6d4a0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"good","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a person or an animal:\nActing in the interest of what is beneficial, ethical, or moral.","Acting in the interest of what is beneficial, ethical, or moral."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["That is good; an elliptical exclamation of satisfaction or commendation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/good"},{"term":"taste","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the sensations produced by the tongue in response to certain chemicals; the quality of giving this sensation.","The sense that consists in the perception and interpretation of this sensation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sample the flavor of something orally.","To have a taste; to excite a particular sensation by which flavor is distinguished."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/taste"}],"entries":[{"title":"Good Taste","blocks":[{"text":"\"Good Taste\" is a science fiction short story by American writer Isaac Asimov. It first appeared in a limited edition book of the same name by Apocalypse Press in 1976. It subsequently appeared in Asimov's Science Fiction and in the 1983 collection The Winds of Change and Other Stories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fc/Good_Taste.jpg/330px-Good_Taste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Good_Taste","wikidata":"Q5582966"},{"title":"Aesthetic taste","blocks":[{"text":"In aesthetics, the concept of taste has been the interest of philosophers such as Plato, Hume, and Kant. It is defined by the ability to make valid judgments about an object's aesthetic value. However, these judgments are deficient in objectivity, creating the 'paradox of taste'. The term 'taste' is used because these judgments are similarly made when one physically tastes food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/David_Hume_Ramsay.jpg/330px-David_Hume_Ramsay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aesthetic_taste","wikidata":"Q1143012"},{"title":"Good","blocks":[{"text":"In most contexts, the concept of good denotes the conduct that should be preferred when posed with a choice between possible actions. Good is generally considered to be the opposite of evil. The meaning and etymology of the term and its associated translations among ancient and contemporary languages show substantial variation in its inflection and meaning, depending on circumstances of place and history, or of philosophical or religious context."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Good","wikidata":"Q15290"},{"title":"Taste","blocks":[{"text":"The gustatory system or sense of taste is the sensory system that is partially responsible for the perception of taste. Taste is the perception stimulated when a substance in the mouth reacts chemically with taste receptor cells located on taste buds in the oral cavity, mostly on the tongue. Taste, along with the sense of smell and trigeminal nerve stimulation, determines flavors of food and other substances. Humans have taste receptors on taste buds and other areas, including the upper surface of the tongue and the epiglottis. The gustatory cortex is responsible for the perception of taste."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Taste_bud.svg/330px-Taste_bud.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taste","wikidata":"Q124794"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/JohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg/500px-JohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/JohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg/1280px-JohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/JohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg/500px-JohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohnWaters-byPhilipRomano.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg/500px-Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg/500px-Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"For Kant, beauty is not a property of any object, but an aesthetic judgement based on a subjective feeling.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AImmanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg/250px-Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg/250px-Lenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In many Abrahamic religions,  angels are considered to be good beings and are contrasted with demons, who are their evil counterparts.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALenz_Entwurf_zu_einem_Engel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Socrates_Pio-Clementino_Inv314.jpg/500px-Socrates_Pio-Clementino_Inv314.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu